latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initial Analysis:\n   a. Create a deep copy of the input grid to work on.\n   b. Calculate the dimensions of the grid.\n   c. Identify all gray (5) cells and store their coordinates.\n   d. Calculate the overall density of gray cells in the grid.\n\n2. Structural Analysis:\n   a. Detect lines (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) of gray cells.\n   b. Identify corners and junctions (cells with 2 or more non-collinear gray neighbors).\n   c. Recognize 2x2 squares of gray cells.\n   d. Detect X-shaped patterns and other common shapes (T, L, etc.).\n   e. Identify isolated gray cells (no gray neighbors).\n\n3. Calculate Structural Importance:\n   For each gray cell, assign an importance score based on:\n   a. Number of gray neighbors (more neighbors = higher score).\n   b. Position in identified structures (corners, junctions, endpoints = higher score).\n   c. Distance from the edge of the grid (closer to edge = higher score).\n   d. Part of a recognized pattern (2x2 square, X shape, etc.).\n\n4. Determine Transformation Threshold:\n   a. Calculate the average structural importance score.\n   b. Adjust this baseline based on the overall gray cell density:\n      - Higher density -> lower threshold (more cells stay gray).\n      - Lower density -> higher threshold (more cells turn blue).\n\n5. Apply Transformation Rules:\n   a. Change all isolated gray cells to blue, except those on the grid edges.\n   b. Transform all 2x2 squares of gray cells entirely to blue.\n   c. For lines:\n      - If 3 cells or shorter, keep endpoints gray and change middle to blue.\n      - If longer, keep 2 cells at each end gray, change the rest to blue.\n   d. For X shapes, keep the center and endpoints gray, change others to blue.\n   e. For remaining cells, change to blue if their importance score is below the threshold.\n\n6. Post-processing:\n   a. Scan the grid to ensure no blue cells are completely surrounded by gray cells.\n      If found, change such blue cells back to gray.\n   b. For each distinct cluster of cells, ensure at least one cell remains gray.\n      If all cells in a cluster are blue, change the most "important" one back to gray.\n\n7. Edge Preservation:\n   a. Scan the edges of the grid.\n   b. For any blue cells on the edge that were originally gray:\n      - If they\'re part of a larger structure extending inwards, change them back to gray.\n      - If they\'re isolated or only connected to one other cell, keep them blue.\n\n8. Final Consistency Check:\n   a. Compare the transformed grid to the original.\n   b. Ensure that the overall structure and key features are maintained.\n   c. Verify that the number of blue cells is reasonable (not too many or too few compared to original gray cells).\n\n9. Return the Transformed Grid:\n   Convert the working grid back to a ColoredGrid object and return it.\n\nThis plan aims to address the complexities observed in the reflection, providing a systematic approach that should work across various input patterns. It balances local cell transformations with global pattern preservation, and includes steps to handle specific patterns consistently while allowing for adaptability to different grid densities and structures.'
